    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Dorian city-state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> valley that conquered Messenia and lived off the labour of state-owned serfs, the helots.</li><li>With two kings, five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> and a citizen army trained from childhood, it was the leading land power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classical-greece">Classical Greece</a>.</li><li>Sparta led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 404 BCE, and lost its power for good at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Classical Sparta had no city walls; for centuries it relied on its army alone and was fortified only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

