    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/homer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homer.jpg" alt="Homer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homer_At_the_British_Museum_2024_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net)., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sparta lay in the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taygetus">Taygetus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parnon">Parnon</a> mountain ranges. The region had been important in the Mycenaean age: a palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayios-vasileios">Ayios Vasileios</a> and a mansion at the Menelaion near the later city date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> made Lacedaemon the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helen-of-troy">Helen</a>. After the collapse of Mycenaean civilisation the valley was thinly settled. Greek tradition explained the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorian Greeks</a> as the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heraclidae</a>, the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, and the two Spartan royal houses, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiads</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurypontid-dynasty">Eurypontids</a>, claimed descent from him. Archaeologically, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlement at Sparta dates to the tenth century BCE. The city was formed by the union of four villages, Pitana, Mesoa, Limnai and Kynosoura, and probably in the eighth century BCE it absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyclae">Amyclae</a>, a few kilometres to the south, as a fifth. Over the following generations the Spartans subjected the rest of Laconia, whose free inhabitants became <em>perioikoi</em>, dwellers around, with local self-government but no voice in Spartan policy. In the late eighth century BCE, by the traditional dating in the First Messenian War of c. 743–724 BCE, they crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-taygetus">Mount Taygetus</a> and conquered Messenia, a step that shaped Spartan society for the next four centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-artemis-orthia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-artemis-orthia.jpg" alt="Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-artemis-orthia">Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artemis_Orthia_location_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">User Bridesmill on en.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Spartan society rested on a sharp division between a small body of full citizens, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a> or <em>homoioi</em>, peers, the free but subordinate perioikoi, and the helots, state-owned serfs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia who worked the land. Each Spartiate drew his income from an estate farmed by helots, who handed over a fixed share of the harvest; citizens were thus freed from labour and barred from trade and crafts, which were left to the perioikoi. Helots greatly outnumbered citizens: at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a> in 479 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> counts seven helots for each of the 5,000 Spartiates. Fear of revolt shaped Spartan life. Each year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> formally declared war on the helots, and the <em>krypteia</em>, a body of young men, could kill helots without penalty. Every citizen had to contribute food to a common mess, the <em>syssition</em>, and those who could not lost full status. Sparta used iron bars rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for internal exchange and struck no coins of its own until the third century BCE. Spartan women had unusual freedom, exercised in public and could own property; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> claimed that they held about two-fifths of the land. Archaic Sparta was less austere than legend suggests: the poet Alcman composed choral songs there in the seventh century BCE, Laconian pottery and bronzes were exported widely and the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-artemis-orthia">Artemis Orthia</a> received rich offerings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae.jpg" alt="Battle of Thermopylae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Battle of Thermopylae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9onidas_aux_Thermopyles_-_Jacques-Louis_David_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_Peintures_INV_3690_;_L_3711.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spartan army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>, heavy infantry fighting in close formation, was the most respected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Boys entered the <em>agoge</em>, the state system of training, at the age of seven, and men served until sixty, devoting themselves to war like no other Greek citizens. In the sixth century BCE Sparta defeated Tegea, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-champions">Battle of the Champions</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> for the borderland of Thyreatis around 546 BCE, and under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-i">Cleomenes I</a> crushed the Argives at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sepeia">Sepeia</a> in 494 BCE. In 480 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a> held the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> with a small Greek force including 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a> until he was outflanked and killed, and in 479 BCE the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> commanded the Greek army that destroyed the Persian land forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> of 431–404 BCE Sparta invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> repeatedly, but the surrender of some 120 Spartiates on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sphacteria">Sphacteria</a> in 425 BCE shocked the Greek world. Victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-418-bce">Battle of Mantinea</a> in 418 BCE restored its reputation, and with Persian money Sparta built a fleet with which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> destroyed the Athenian navy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aegospotami">Aegospotami</a> in 405 BCE. Its fatal weakness was manpower: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> put the citizen body at about 8,000 men in 480 BCE, but only about 700 Spartiates fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Battle of Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE, and some 400 of them died.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-rhetra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-rhetra.jpg" alt="Great Rhetra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-rhetra">Great Rhetra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SpartaGreatRhetra.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Publius97, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spartan constitution was admired in antiquity for its stability and its mixture of monarchy, aristocracy and democracy. Two hereditary kings, one from each royal house, commanded armies and held priestly duties. A council of elders, the <em>gerousia</em>, of 28 men over the age of sixty elected for life, sat with the kings, prepared business for the assembly and judged major cases. The assembly of all adult <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a>, the <em>apella</em>, decided on proposals put to it and elected officials by shouting. Five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a>, chosen each year from the whole citizen body, supervised the kings, the training of the young and the control of the helots, and came to hold great power; two of them accompanied a king on campaign. The Spartans attributed this system and their whole way of life to the lawgiver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycurgus-of-sparta">Lycurgus</a>, whose existence and date are uncertain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> preserves an early text, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-rhetra">Great Rhetra</a>, presented as an oracle setting out the roles of kings, elders and people. Abroad, Sparta led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a>, a network of alliances with Corinth, Tegea, Elis and other states formed from the mid-sixth century BCE, through which it supported friendly oligarchies, contained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and later opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Its officials abroad, the harmosts, governed cities under Spartan control after 404 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/life-of-lycurgus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/life-of-lycurgus.jpg" alt="Life of Lycurgus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/life-of-lycurgus">Life of Lycurgus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plutarch,_Parallel_Lives,_Oxford,_MS._Canonici_Greek_93_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Plutarch, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 404 BCE made Sparta, for a generation, the leading power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and the stand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> became one of the most famous episodes in ancient history. Its deeper influence was as an idea. Ancient writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, in his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-lacedaemonians">Constitution of the Lacedaemonians</a></em>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> discussed Sparta as a model, or a warning, of a state that shaped its citizens through law and education. The French scholar François Ollier named this idealised picture the Spartan mirage, since much of it was projected by Spartans and their admirers. Plutarch's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-lycurgus">Life of Lycurgus</a></em> influenced later political thinkers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>, and revolutionaries in eighteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> cited Spartan austerity and civic virtue. In the nineteenth and twentieth centuries Sparta was held up as a model of military education, and Nazi ideologists admired its infanticide and racial hierarchy. The English words laconic, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, and spartan preserve its reputation for brevity and hardship. Sparta's only significant overseas colony, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a>, modern Taranto in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, was founded by tradition in 706 BCE. The sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-artemis-orthia">Artemis Orthia</a>, where in the Roman period visitors watched youths endure ritual whipping in a purpose-built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, shows how Sparta's traditions later became a spectacle for outsiders.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra.jpg" alt="Battle of Leuctra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Battle of Leuctra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelopidas_leading_the_Thebans_at_the_battle_of_Leuctra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edmund Ollier Publication date 1882, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Spartan hegemony after 404 BCE proved short. Its rule through harmosts and narrow oligarchies alienated former allies, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> of 395–387 BCE Thebes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> fought Sparta with Persian support, while a Persian fleet destroyed the Spartan navy at Cnidus in 394 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a> of 387/386 BCE restored Spartan dominance on the mainland under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a>, but the seizure of the Theban citadel in 382 BCE provoked a war that ended in disaster. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Battle of Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE the Thebans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> defeated the Spartan army and killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus I</a>, and in 370–369 BCE they invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, freed Messenia and founded the city of Messene, taking away half of Sparta's helot land. Sparta never recovered. It stayed out of the League of Corinth, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agis-iii">Agis III</a>'s revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> at Megalopolis in 331 BCE. The reforming kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agis-iv">Agis IV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> tried to rebuild the citizen body by cancelling debts and redistributing land, but Cleomenes was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Sellasia</a> in 222 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> and the Achaean League. After the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabis">Nabis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a> forced Sparta into the Achaean League in 192 BCE. Sparta's attempts to leave the league helped provoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaean-war">Achaean War</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroyed Corinth in 146 BCE Sparta became a free city allied to Rome.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sparta was the leading land power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classical-greece">Classical Greece</a>: its citizen soldiers, supported by an enslaved helot population, led Greek resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, and its idealised discipline shaped Western political thought long after its power collapsed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE.</em></p>

